# Every setting this project has, in one file.
#
# The worker reads this file directly: worker/env.py finds the nearest .env walking
# up from the module, which is the repository root.
#
# The web app does not read it in production — Vercel holds its values, and the
# table below says which ones it needs there. `next dev` looks for .env in
# apps/web/, so a local dev server needs a copy or a symlink; nothing else does.
#
#   worker only   TG_*, TELEGRAM_OPS_CHAT, TELEGRAM_OPS_TOKEN
#   web only      + WA_VERIFY_TOKEN, WA_APP_SECRET
#   both          + WA_PHONE_NUMBER_ID, WA_ACCESS_TOKEN
#   web only      TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET, TELEGRAM_ADMIN_CHAT, ADMIN_*, STRIPE_*
#   both          DATABASE_URL, TELEGRAM_TOKEN, TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME, SITE_URL

# ── the database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The worker and the web want DIFFERENT ports on the same database, and this is
# not a detail to normalise away:
#
#   worker  session pooler, port 5432. It takes a session-scoped advisory lock to
#           stop two runs overlapping, and a transaction pooler cannot hold one —
#           the lock would report success and guard nothing.
#   web     transaction pooler, port 6543. No session locks, many short-lived
#           connections, which is what that pooler is for.
#
# Never the direct host (db.<ref>.supabase.co): it publishes an IPv6 address and no
# IPv4 one, so it resolves where IPv6 works and fails outright where it does not —
# with nothing changed on our side.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# ── Telegram: our bot ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# From @BotFather. The worker sends alerts with it and the web answers commands
# with it, so it goes in both places.
TELEGRAM_TOKEN=
# Without the @. Builds the t.me links: the form's "Connect Telegram" button and
# the upgrade button on a free-tier alert.
TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=
# Any long random string. Passed to setWebhook and checked on every update, so that
# only Telegram can post to the webhook route.
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Where breakage is reported: a chat id, in practice your own chat with the bot.
# Find it by messaging @userinfobot. The bot cannot write to you until you have
# pressed Start on it — Telegram answers 403 until then, and the alert is lost.
TELEGRAM_OPS_CHAT=
# The bot that carries those alerts. Optional: left empty, TELEGRAM_TOKEN is used
# and operational warnings arrive in the same conversation as customer alerts.
# A separate bot keeps them apart, and it can be polled with getUpdates to read
# a chat id — which the customer bot cannot, because its webhook owns updates.
# Only the token matters; whose account created the bot in @BotFather does not.
TELEGRAM_OPS_TOKEN=
# The one chat allowed to run admin commands in the bot. Your own chat id.
TELEGRAM_ADMIN_CHAT=

# ── Telegram: reading the feed ────────────────────────────────────────────
# From https://my.telegram.org -> API development tools. These identify the
# application, not the account.
TG_API_ID=
TG_API_HASH=
# A live login to a PERSONAL account, printed once by `python -m worker login`.
# Treat it like a password. A bot session will not do: Telegram forbids bots from
# reading history, which is what this is for.
TG_SESSION=
# The chat to read, without the @.
TG_WATCH=
# Names this reader. The ingest cursor and the per-reader uniqueness are keyed by
# it, so two accounts must not share a name — and reusing a name across accounts
# makes the reader skip everything, silently.
TG_READER=
# Words replaced with "hs" in stored message bodies, comma separated. The source
# signs every message with its own brand, and that name has no business sitting in
# our database, our backups, or any report drawn from them.
TG_REDACT=

# ── the site ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where this app is reachable, no trailing slash. Used in every link the bot sends
# and in the upgrade page's URLs, so the worker needs it too.
SITE_URL=

# ── the admin console ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate both with:  node scripts/admin-hash.mjs '<your password>'
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH is a scrypt hash, not the password — the password itself is
# stored nowhere, including here. Without it the console has no way in, which is
# the safe direction for a missing secret to fail in.
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
# Signs the session cookie AND salts the visitor and login-attempt hashes. At least
# 32 characters. Changing it signs everybody out and makes yesterday's visitor
# hashes unrelated to today's, which is harmless.
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=

# ── Stripe ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Card payments. Inert unless STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is set: the deployment runs
# perfectly well with no way to pay, which is the state it starts in.
#
# There is deliberately no price here. A price belongs to a plan, so it lives in
# plans.stripe_price_id — adding a plan is then a row in the database rather than a
# deploy, and a plan whose row says thirty days can no longer be charged for seven.
# Which Stripe account is in use. Anything other than the word "live" means the
# test one, so taking real money is opt-in and reverting is the same variable.
#
# The keys say which account they belong to, and a mismatch is refused rather
# than used: live mode holding a test key takes no money and looks like it
# worked, and test mode holding a live key charges a real card.
STRIPE_MODE=test

# The test account.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# From the webhook's own page in the Stripe dashboard, not the API keys page.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The live account. Filled in now, used only when STRIPE_MODE=live. Each account
# has its own webhook endpoint, so this is a different signing secret — and its
# own Price ids, which live in plans.stripe_price_id_live.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY_LIVE=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET_LIVE=

# ── WhatsApp ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Messages come from a WhatsApp Business account through Meta's Cloud API, so the
# sender is a business name rather than somebody's phone number.
#
# Two ways a listing can be sent, and the difference is not cosmetic:
#
#   * within 24 hours of the person's last message, free-form text with a link
#     preview — the same message Telegram gets, photo included, and free;
#   * outside it, only an approved template. Fixed shape, no photo, and Meta
#     charges per message.
#
# `user_channels.last_inbound_at` is what decides which, per recipient.
#
# WA_TEMPLATE_NAME is the template approved in the WhatsApp Manager; see the
# instructions for the exact body to paste. Leave it empty and listings outside
# the window stay queued rather than being sent as something Meta would refuse.
#
# WA_LINK_PREFIX is where the template's button points. A template button is a
# fixed prefix plus one variable, so the three portals cannot be linked directly:
# /l/<id> on our own domain redirects, and counts the click.
WA_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
WA_ACCESS_TOKEN=
WA_TEMPLATE_NAME=new_listing
WA_TEMPLATE_LANGUAGE=en
WA_LINK_PREFIX=https://londonhomefinder.co.uk/l

# Whether the approved template declares a header of type IMAGE.
#
# WhatsApp makes its own thumbnail for a link preview — small, heavily
# compressed, no setting to change it — and a template carries no preview at
# all. So a listing with a picture is sent as a real image instead: inside the
# 24-hour window as an image message with the text as its caption, outside it as
# the template's image header.
#
# Leave this empty until the template has been re-approved WITH that header.
# Sending a component a template does not declare is error 132000, and every
# alert would fail.
WA_TEMPLATE_IMAGE=

# Web only. WA_VERIFY_TOKEN is any string you choose, given to Meta when the
# webhook is registered and echoed back once to prove the endpoint is yours.
# WA_APP_SECRET signs every delivery: without checking it, anyone who learns the
# url can claim to be any number.
WA_VERIFY_TOKEN=
WA_APP_SECRET=

# The number the site's "Connect to WhatsApp" button opens, digits only, country
# code first, no plus. Web only.
#
# Leave WA_PHONE_NUMBER_ID empty and the button does not appear at all, which is
# the state to be in until a business is registered: Meta's test number reaches
# five recipients you add by hand, and nobody else.
#
# The two messengers are unconnected. Signing up on both makes two accounts with
# two plans, and the free trial applies to each — recognising that it is one
# person would mean asking for a phone number or keeping a cookie, and neither is
# a fair price for policing two days.
WHATSAPP_NUMBER=

# The template for "your plan has ended, here is the button", used only when the
# 24-hour window is shut — which is exactly when that notice is due — and only
# for a plan somebody paid for. An ended trial is told nothing there: this is a
# marketing message at three times the price of one inside the window, and the
# warning before the end has already gone out while the window was open. See
# `worth_a_template` in the outbox.
#
# Leave empty and nobody with a shut window is told at all. Submit it in
# WhatsApp Manager with no variables in the body and one dynamic url button:
#
#   Body:
#     Your alerts have stopped — your plan has run out.
#
#     Your filter is kept exactly as it is. Tap below and everything turns
#     back on, with nothing to set up again.
#
#   Button: Visit website, Dynamic, "Get full access"
#     https://londonhomefinder.co.uk/upgrade?t={{1}}   sample: AbC123xyz
#
# One wording for both ways a period ends — out of days and out of its message
# allowance — which is why the body needs no variable. The button's does carry
# one: the upgrade token, without which the page cannot tell whose plan is
# being bought. The language must be plain `en` and not `en_GB`, or every send
# is refused with 132001.
#
# That button shape is the one that is live, not the one that is best — see
# WA_NOTICE_BUTTON below, which is where the quick-reply version is described
# and why it is worth having.
WA_NOTICE_TEMPLATE=

# Which of Meta's rates that template is billed at: `utility` or `marketing`.
# Nothing is sent differently — this only prices the row in `wa_messages`, and
# the gap is wide: $0.0635 against $0.022 for everything else.
#
# **Leave it empty.** It defaults to `utility`, which is what Meta's latest
# review returned. The history is the point: submitted as utility, came back
# marketing, reviewed as utility again — Meta classifies templates itself and
# re-classifies them in both directions, which is why this is a setting and
# not a constant. It exists to follow Meta, not to be remembered on every
# deploy. The current answer is on the template itself:
#
#   curl -s "https://graph.facebook.com/v21.0/$WABA_ID/message_templates\
# ?fields=name,status,category" -H "Authorization: Bearer $WA_ACCESS_TOKEN"
#
# Anything that is not one of the two words falls back to `utility` rather
# than reaching the ledger: a row the invoice has no such line for cannot be
# reconciled at all.
WA_NOTICE_CATEGORY=

# Which button that template was approved with: `url` or `reply`. Unlike the
# category, this one changes what is sent — and it is about delivery rather
# than money.
#
# `url` is the original and the default: the button carries the checkout link
# with the upgrade token as its one variable. It also sends us nothing when
# tapped, so the 24-hour window stays shut through the payment — Meta refuses
# the receipt with 131047, and every alert the person just paid for sits in
# the queue until they write in of their own accord.
#
# `reply` is a quick reply, which *is* an inbound message: the window reopens
# on the tap, before the payment rather than after, and the site answers with
# the same checkout link. No token travels with it — the link is minted when
# the tap arrives.
#
# **A template's buttons are declared, so this and the live template move
# together.** The wrong shape is 132000 on every notice. Submit the
# quick-reply template, wait for APPROVED, check the category, then set this.
# See docs/whatsapp-live.md step 4.
WA_NOTICE_BUTTON=

# ── the payment receipt ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The name of the approved template that tells somebody their payment went
# through when WhatsApp's 24-hour window is shut. **Set on Vercel, not on the
# server**: the Stripe webhook is the site's, not the worker's.
#
# Why a template is needed for a receipt at all: a plan ends after a day of
# silence, and a day of silence is what shuts the window. So the cohort most
# likely to pay is precisely the cohort that cannot be sent free-form text,
# and before this the receipt was attempted anyway, refused with 131047, and
# logged as "payment granted but not announced". Worse than the missing
# receipt: the alerts they had just bought stayed queued behind the same shut
# window until they happened to write in.
#
# A template reaches a shut window but does not reopen it — only something
# inbound does — which is why this one carries a quick reply. The tap is what
# releases the queue; the words only explain why it is there.
#
#   Name: payment_received     Category: UTILITY     Language: English (en)
#
#   Body:
#     Payment received — thank you. Your plan is extended and every matching
#     listing is on its way again.
#
#     Tap below and I will show you what you are on and what you are looking
#     for.
#
#   Button: Quick reply, "Show my plan"
#
# No body variable, on purpose: the one fact a receipt wants to carry is the
# date the plan runs to, and that is null on a plan with no end date — an
# empty parameter is refused, so a wording that needs none cannot fail that
# way. The language must be plain `en` and not `en_GB`, or every send is
# refused with 132001.
#
# Left empty, the receipt is simply not sent when the window is shut, and the
# log says this variable is why rather than reporting a refusal from Meta.
WA_RECEIPT_TEMPLATE=

# ── a second Telegram reader ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Not here. One file per extra account, in /etc/london-home-finder/readers/,
# holding only what differs — TG_READER, TG_SESSION and, if the account has its
# own, TG_API_ID and TG_API_HASH. Then:
#   systemctl enable --now london-home-finder-reader@<name>.timer

# ── reading the portals directly ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Rightmove, Zoopla and OpenRent are fetched from their own search pages.
#
# Why curl_cffi and not a plain client: Rightmove answers urllib 403 under any
# User-Agent and answers curl_cffi 200 from the same address, because the rule
# is on the TLS and HTTP/2 fingerprint. Measured 26 September 2026.

# Which browser to impersonate, in preference order, tried in turn when one is
# refused. Empty uses the built-in list — currently chrome150, firefox147,
# chrome146, safari2601.
#
# This is worth changing before reaching for a proxy, and it is worth checking
# every few months whether the built-in list has gone stale. It had: until
# 12 October 2026 it named chrome124 (April 2024) and edge101 (April 2022), so
# every request claimed to be a browser two to four years old, which is one of
# the cheapest signals Cloudflare has. The targets available come from the
# installed curl_cffi, not from this file:
#
#   python -c "import typing; from curl_cffi.requests.impersonate import \
#     BrowserTypeLiteral as B; print(sorted(typing.get_args(B)))"
#
# One name here sets the user agent, the client hints, the TLS fingerprint and
# the HTTP/2 settings frame together. Do not try to set a User-Agent separately
# anywhere: a UA that disagrees with the handshake is a worse signal than an
# old UA, which is why there is no variable for one.
SCRAPE_IMPERSONATE=

# ── the residential proxy ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The three portals do not agree about this server. Measured from the server
# itself on 27 September 2026:
#
#   Rightmove    serves it              — no proxy needed, and none is used
#   Zoopla       403 under every fingerprint
#   OpenRent     405 under every fingerprint
#
# From a home connection all three serve the identical requests, so what Zoopla
# and OpenRent object to is the address, not the request.
#
# The proxy is therefore an escalation, not a route for everything: each host
# is tried directly first and the proxy is the answer to being refused. So
# Rightmove — the largest share of the traffic — stays free, and only the two
# that block us cost anything. A refusal is remembered for the run, so only the
# first district pays the wasted probe; it is forgotten afterwards, so a portal
# that stops blocking us goes back to being free by itself.
#
# Leave it empty and nothing changes except that Zoopla and OpenRent keep
# failing, which is honest rather than silent: the run logs "refused this
# server's address and no proxy is configured".
#
# ── one reader opts out of this entirely ────────────────────────────────
#
# SpareRoom is pinned to a direct route in code — `SpareRoom.direct_only`, via
# `sweep._pin_direct` — so setting SCRAPE_PROXY does *not* put its traffic on
# the meter. Measured from a desk on 8 and 9 October 2026 it serves every page
# 200 under chrome124, with no Cloudflare, DataDome or Akamai anywhere in the
# headers, so there is nothing here to pay around.
#
# Whether it serves the *server's* address is untested. If it stops, the scrape
# stage records `refused_and_pinned` and the run log carries a warn, and the
# reader simply stops reading rather than quietly starting to cost money.
# Deciding to pay for it is then a code change, which is the point: a silent
# escalation is a bill nobody agreed to.
#
# For DataImpulse, from their dashboard's "Get proxy" panel:
#
#   SCRAPE_PROXY=http://<login>:<password>@gw.dataimpulse.com:823
#
# Port 823 changes the exit address on every request. To hold one address
# across a sweep use their sticky port instead (10000-10500 in the dashboard),
# and to pin the country append it to the login as they document:
#
#   SCRAPE_PROXY=http://<login>__cr.gb:<password>@gw.dataimpulse.com:823
#
# ── which sticky port, and why it barely matters ────────────────────────
#
# Any one of 10000-10500: each is a separate session and so a separate exit
# address, and naming one here only tells the fetcher that this is a sticky
# setup rather than the rotating port. Which port a run actually uses is drawn
# per reader and then *remembered across runs* — see SCRAPE_STATE_DIR — so each
# reader holds its own address for as long as the portal serves it, and no two
# readers pile onto one. Expect the invoice to show ports you did not configure.
#
# Sticky is strongly preferable to the rotating port here, and the reason is the
# cookie rather than the address: a `__cf_bm` is issued against the peer that
# asked for it, so an address that changes every request is an address that can
# never present one. Port 823 therefore gives up the warm session this is all
# built around.
#
# Traffic is metered either way and written to every scrape run's counters as
# `proxy_bytes` — libcurl's own transfer figure, compressed, in the same units
# DataImpulse invoices. `bytes` is the run's whole transfer including what went
# out directly. The two let the bill be checked rather than trusted:
#
#   SELECT date_trunc('day', started_at) AS day, source_key,
#          pg_size_pretty(sum((counters->>'bytes')::bigint))       AS all_traffic,
#          pg_size_pretty(sum((counters->>'proxy_bytes')::bigint)) AS billable
#     FROM job_stages
#    WHERE stage = 'scrape'
#      AND jsonb_typeof(counters->'proxy_bytes') = 'number'
#      AND started_at > now() - interval '7 days'
#    GROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY 1 DESC, 2;
SCRAPE_PROXY=

# ── the second provider, for when the first pool goes bad ─────────────────
#
# Empty, and empty is the normal state. Filling it in is the whole of
# switching the route on: no other variable, no code change, no migration.
# Leaving it empty costs nothing anywhere — no pool is built, no second state
# file is read, and every route list is the length it was.
#
# ── what it is for, which is not "more exits" ────────────────────────────
#
# The primary route already tries three of its vendor's exit addresses, on the
# measured evidence that a refused exit stays refused while a different one
# serves the identical request minutes later. That only helps while *some*
# address in the pool is being served. By the time `SCRAPE_ATTEMPTS` is gone
# what has been established is about the vendor — its pool, this hour, for
# this portal — and the next thing worth varying is whose addresses they are.
#
# So this is a different company, not a different port. Smart Proxy is the
# intended one; their gateway url goes here in the same shape DataImpulse's
# does, and the sticky-port reasoning above applies identically — a sticky
# port is what lets the exit hold a `__cf_bm` across runs, and the rotating
# port gives that up.
#
#   SCRAPE_PROXY_FALLBACK=http://<login>:<password>@gate.smartproxy.net:10000
#
# ── what it costs, stated plainly ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Three more requests, on top of the five above, so the worst case for one
# page becomes eight. That is more than `SCRAPE_ATTEMPTS` advertises and it is
# deliberate: those three are reachable only when this machine's address,
# three exits of one vendor and three of another all refuse the same page —
# a run that was going to fail anyway, where the eighth request is the one
# that might stop it failing.
#
# The traffic is metered apart from the primary's and reported as
# `fallback_bytes` rather than folded into `proxy_bytes`, because two vendors
# send two invoices and a figure mixing them could be reconciled against
# neither. A non-zero `fallback_requests` also earns a `warn` in the run log:
# it means the first pool spent everything and still did not get the page,
# which once in a while is one bad exit and every run is a vendor to have a
# conversation with.
#
#   SELECT date_trunc('day', s.started_at) AS day, r.job,
#          count(*) FILTER (WHERE s.counters ? 'fallback_requests') AS fell_back,
#          count(*)                                                AS runs,
#          pg_size_pretty(sum((s.counters->>'proxy_bytes')::bigint))    AS primary_bill,
#          pg_size_pretty(sum((s.counters->>'fallback_bytes')::bigint)) AS second_bill
#     FROM job_stages s JOIN job_runs r ON r.id = s.run_id
#    WHERE s.stage = 'scrape' AND s.started_at > now() - interval '7 days'
#    GROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY 1 DESC, 2;
SCRAPE_PROXY_FALLBACK=

# Attempts the fallback gets, on top of SCRAPE_ATTEMPTS. Empty uses 3.
#
# Read separately from the url so the route can be switched off without
# losing the url, which is what somebody bisecting a bad afternoon wants:
# `SCRAPE_FALLBACK_ATTEMPTS=0` stops paying the second vendor and leaves the
# configuration intact.
#
# Three rather than one because one attempt makes the fallback a coin toss on
# a single exit, and the whole reason there is a pool is that exits vary.
SCRAPE_FALLBACK_ATTEMPTS=

# Attempts for one page before calling it refused, both routes together.
# Empty uses 5.
#
# One budget rather than two, which is what changed on 12 October 2026. This
# variable used to be `SCRAPE_EXITS` and named only the paid route's ladder,
# while the free route separately tried every browser fingerprint in the list —
# so a page that failed everywhere cost four requests plus three, and nobody
# reading either number would have said seven. `SCRAPE_EXITS` is still read if
# it is set, but it is the whole budget now, so a box with `SCRAPE_EXITS=3`
# will make fewer attempts than before, not more.
#
# At most two of the five go on the free route. A 403 from this machine's own
# address could be a fingerprint rule or an address rule, and two attempts
# under different engines can tell them apart because a rule that catches
# chrome150 will not catch firefox147. The third and fourth would be more
# requests from an address that has already said no twice, spending the budget
# the paid route needs to actually get the page.
#
# Attempts are spaced rather than fired as a burst: 0.5s before the first
# retry, doubling to a 4s cap, and a `Retry-After` the portal names wins over
# that when it is longer. At most about 7.5s across a fully failing ladder.
#
# A rotation that worked is not a fault and logs nothing. What it leaves is a
# counter, and the rate is the thing to watch — once a week is a pool with the
# odd bad exit in it, every run is a pool going bad:
#
#   SELECT date_trunc('day', s.started_at) AS day, r.job,
#          sum((s.counters->>'exit_rotations')::int) AS rotations,
#          count(*) FILTER (WHERE s.status = 'degraded') AS still_lost
#     FROM job_stages s JOIN job_runs r ON r.id = s.run_id
#    WHERE s.stage = 'scrape' AND s.started_at > now() - interval '7 days'
#    GROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY 1 DESC, 2;
SCRAPE_ATTEMPTS=

# Where the scrapers keep what has to outlive one run, which is a cookie jar
# per exit address and a note of which exit address was last served. Empty is
# right on the server and on a laptop both.
#
# ── why there is anything to keep ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Measured against Zoopla's live London search page on 12 October 2026: it is
# served by Cloudflare, and it issues `__cf_bm` — Cloudflare's bot-management
# token — with an 1800 second expiry. A request presenting a jar gets one
# Set-Cookie back; a cold one gets four. Three separate processes sharing a jar
# kept one visitor id throughout.
#
# The readers are `Type=oneshot` systemd units, so a run is a process: without
# a file, every run five minutes apart arrived as a visitor Cloudflare had
# never seen, from a residential address it had never seen either, because the
# exit was drawn afresh each time. A 30-minute token against a 5-minute timer
# covers six runs, and that is only reachable from disk.
#
# ── where it goes if this is empty ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `$STATE_DIRECTORY` on the server, which systemd sets from
# `StateDirectory=london-home-finder` in the unit, so there is nothing to
# configure there. That line in the unit is load-bearing: `ProtectSystem=strict`
# makes the filesystem read-only, `ProtectHome=true` hides the fallback, and
# `PrivateTmp=true` gives each invocation its own /tmp — so a store under /tmp
# would be wiped between exactly the runs it is meant to bridge.
#
# Off the server it falls back to the user's cache directory, which is what the
# Windows desk and a laptop want. Set this to point a run by hand at a scratch
# directory instead. Nothing in here is a secret and nothing cannot be rebuilt
# by running again, so it is not worth backing up — but deleting it costs the
# next run its warm session.
SCRAPE_STATE_DIR=

# Seconds of random delay before each request, drawn fresh every time. Empty
# uses 0.4; 0 turns it off. Seconds, not milliseconds, to match every other
# delay in the worker — a tenth is 0.1.
#
# It removes the one pattern a reused handle creates: the pages of a sweep
# follow each other as fast as the portal can answer, twenty-six requests in
# one run as measured on 10 October 2026. The run cadence — the part a portal
# could read off a stopwatch — is handled by the timers instead, which offset
# the three readers by a third of their interval.
#
# Four tenths because the variance is what matters and not the delay. Six
# sequential fetches of Zoopla's London search page from a home connection,
# measured the same day with this off, took 435/186/182/159/193/358ms: a median
# of 190ms and a standard deviation of 115ms with nothing added at all. So tens
# of milliseconds would disappear into the connection's own noise, and seconds
# would be paying for variance we already have. `uniform(0, 0.4)` has a
# standard deviation of exactly 115ms, which doubles the variance of the series
# — as much irregularity again as the network provides for free.
#
# Not a rate limit. The mean cost is half this: 0.2s on a two-page read, 5s on
# the largest sweep seen, 18s at the sweep's own ceilings, and 80s on the one
# run that could ask for 400 pages — `openrent` working through a slug backlog,
# which runs from the Windows desk with no execution limit anyway. Set it to 0
# when running a reader by hand and waiting for the output.
SCRAPE_JITTER=

# Hosts that never go through the proxy, whatever happens, comma separated.
# Pictures are the reason: a preview image is an already-resized CDN variant
# served to any ordinary client, so sending it through paid residential traffic
# is the most expensive way to fetch the cheapest thing. In practice we do not
# download pictures at all — the url is stored and Telegram and WhatsApp fetch
# it from their own servers — but this makes sure of it either way.
#
# A reader can add to this list for itself and does not need the variable
# changed: see the SpareRoom note above. Anything named here is added to, never
# in place of, what a reader pins.
SCRAPE_DIRECT_HOSTS=media.rightmove.co.uk,lid.zoocdn.com,lc.zoocdn.com,st.zoocdn.com,imagescdn.openrent.co.uk
